IMPORT `aa-types`

-- Apportionment Act 1928 (Singapore) — ss 1-7, the whole Act.
--
-- Source: ../source/AA1928.txt lines 48-110 (SSO PDF, current version as at
-- 05 Oct 2026, 2020 Revised Edition). The Legislative History lists no
-- amending Act since 1928, only revised editions, so no date is gated.

§ `Apportionment Act 1928`

§§ `Long title`

-- INERT. "An Act for the better apportionment of rents and other periodical
-- payments." [28 December 1928]
GIVETH A STRING
`long title` MEANS "An Act for the better apportionment of rents and other periodical payments."

§§ `1. Short title`

-- INERT. "This Act is the Apportionment Act 1928."
GIVETH A STRING
`s 1 — short title` MEANS "This Act is the Apportionment Act 1928."

§§ `2. Interpretation, and 3. Rents, etc., to accrue from day to day`

-- s 3: "All rents, annuities, dividends and other periodical payments in the
-- nature of income (whether reserved or made payable under an instrument in
-- writing or otherwise) shall, like interest on money lent, be considered as
-- accruing from day to day, and shall be apportionable in respect of time
-- accordingly."
-- s 2: "dividends" ... "does not include payments in the nature of a return
-- or reimbursement of capital".
-- s 6: "Nothing in this Act shall render apportionable any annual sums made
-- payable in policies of assurance of any description."
-- s 7: "This Act shall not extend to any case in which it is expressly
-- stipulated that no apportionment shall take place."
@ref Apportionment Act 1928 s 3
GIVEN p IS A Payment
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `periodical income within s 3` p IF
    CONSIDER p's `kind`
    WHEN `rent`                                              THEN TRUE
    WHEN `annuity`                                           THEN TRUE
    WHEN `dividend`                                          THEN NOT "s 2: not a return or reimbursement of capital" ... p's `a return or reimbursement of capital`
    WHEN `another periodical payment in the nature of income` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `a payment that is not periodical income`           THEN FALSE

@ref Apportionment Act 1928 ss 3, 6, 7
GIVEN p IS A Payment
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `apportionable under the Act` p IF
        "s 3" ... `periodical income within s 3` p
    AND NOT "s 6" ... p's `an annual sum payable under a policy of assurance`
    AND NOT "s 7" ... p's `expressly stipulated that no apportionment shall take place`

-- Why a payment is not apportionable, in the Act's terms. A total enum: the
-- Act names each exclusion.
-- (type declared in aa-types.l4)

§§ `The accrued part — "accruing from day to day"`

-- "like interest on money lent, ... accruing from day to day": the part that
-- has accrued at a cut-off day is the entire portion times the days of the
-- accrual period that have run, over the days in the period.
-- s 2 says the same for dividends in terms: "deemed to have accrued by equal
-- daily increment during and within the period for or in respect of which
-- the payment ... is declared".
--
-- FORK F1: days are counted as whole days elapsed since the period began —
-- the first day not counted, the cut-off day counted — so a period from
-- 1 January to 1 April has 90 days and a cut-off on 1 February is 31 of them.
-- The Act does not say how to count; this is the reading that makes the
-- whole period add up to the entire portion.
@ref Apportionment Act 1928 s 3; s 2 "dividends"
GIVEN p      IS A Payment
      cutoff IS A DATE
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the part of` p `accrued at` cutoff MEANS
    IF   cutoff LESS THAN p's `accrual period begins` OR cutoff GREATER THAN p's `accrual period ends`
    THEN REFUSE "the cut-off day is outside the accrual period of this portion; apportion the portion that was running on that day"
    ELSE IF   `days in the period` AT MOST 0
         THEN REFUSE "the accrual period has no days in it"
         ELSE p's `amount of the entire portion` TIMES `days run` DIVIDED BY `days in the period`
    WHERE
        `days in the period` MEANS Day (p's `accrual period ends`) MINUS Day (p's `accrual period begins`)
        `days run`           MEANS Day cutoff MINUS Day (p's `accrual period begins`)

-- s 5(1): the remedies are "allowing proportionate parts of all just
-- allowances": the same fraction of the allowances comes off.
@ref Apportionment Act 1928 s 5(1)
GIVEN p      IS A Payment
      cutoff IS A DATE
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the recoverable part of` p `accrued at` cutoff MEANS
        `the part of` p `accrued at` cutoff
    MINUS p's `just allowances against the entire portion` TIMES `the fraction`
    WHERE
        `the fraction` MEANS
            IF   p's `amount of the entire portion` EQUALS 0
            THEN 0
            ELSE `the part of` p `accrued at` cutoff DIVIDED BY p's `amount of the entire portion`

§§ `4. Apportioned part of rent, etc., to be payable when next entire portion due`

-- s 4: "The apportioned part ... shall be payable or recoverable — (a) in the
-- case of a continuing rent, annuity, or other such payment, when the entire
-- portion of which such apportioned part forms part becomes due and payable,
-- and not before; and (b) in the case of a rent, annuity, or other such
-- payment determined by re-entry, death, or otherwise, when the next entire
-- portion of the same would have been payable if the same had not so
-- determined, and not before."
-- Either way the day is the day the entire portion is (or would have been)
-- due; the limb is recorded so a reader sees which applies.
@ref Apportionment Act 1928 s 4
GIVEN p IS A Payment
GIVETH A STRING
`the s 4 limb for` p MEANS
    CONSIDER p's `state`
    WHEN `continuing`                                THEN "s 4(a): when the entire portion becomes due and payable, and not before"
    WHEN `determined by re-entry, death or otherwise` THEN "s 4(b): when the next entire portion would have been payable, and not before"

@ref Apportionment Act 1928 s 4
GIVEN p IS A Payment
      d IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the apportioned part of` p `is payable on` d IF
    d AT LEAST p's `entire portion due on`

§§ `The whole question`

@ref Apportionment Act 1928 ss 2-7
GIVEN p      IS A Payment
      cutoff IS A DATE
GIVETH AN `Apportionment outcome`
`the apportionment of` p `at` cutoff MEANS
    BRANCH IF NOT `periodical income within s 3` p
              THEN IF   p's `kind` EQUALS `dividend`
                   THEN `not apportionable: a return or reimbursement of capital (s 2)`
                   ELSE `not apportionable: not periodical income within s 3`
           IF p's `an annual sum payable under a policy of assurance`
              THEN `not apportionable: an annual sum under a policy of assurance (s 6)`
           IF p's `expressly stipulated that no apportionment shall take place`
              THEN `not apportionable: apportionment expressly excluded (s 7)`
           OTHERWISE `apportioned` (`the recoverable part of` p `accrued at` cutoff)
                                   (p's `entire portion due on`)
                                   (`the s 4 limb for` p)

§§ `5. Remedies for recovering apportioned parts`

-- s 5(1): "All persons and their respective executors, administrators and
-- assigns, and also the executors, administrators and assigns respectively of
-- persons whose interests determine with their own deaths, shall have such
-- or the same remedies for recovering such apportioned parts ... as they
-- respectively would have had for recovering the entire portions".
@ref Apportionment Act 1928 s 5(1)
GIVEN c IS A Claimant
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 5(1) gives remedies to` c IF
    NOT c EQUALS `someone else`

-- s 5(2): "Persons liable to pay rents reserved out of or charged on lands or
-- tenements, and the same lands or tenements, shall not be resorted to for
-- any such apportioned part forming part of an entire or continuing rent ...
-- specifically, but the entire or continuing rent, including such apportioned
-- part, shall be recovered and received by the person who, if the rent had
-- not been apportionable ..., would have been entitled to the entire or
-- continuing rent, and the apportioned part shall be recoverable from such
-- person by the executors or other parties entitled under this Act to the
-- same by suit."
-- (type declared in aa-types.l4)

-- FORK F2 (revised 2026-10-05 after the independent test pass): s 5(2)
-- reaches rent reserved out of or charged on land whether it is continuing
-- or has determined. "an entire or continuing rent" is disjunctive: "entire"
-- does its work in the s 4(b) case, the classic one being a life-tenant
-- landlord who dies mid-period — the rent is "determined by ... death" as to
-- them, the next entire rent is received by their successor, and their
-- executors recover the apportioned part from that person by suit. The first
-- reading (continuing rent only) left "entire" with nothing to do.
@ref Apportionment Act 1928 s 5
GIVEN p IS A Payment
GIVETH A `Route of recovery`
`the route for recovering the apportioned part of` p MEANS
    IF       p's `kind` EQUALS `rent`
         AND p's `reserved out of or charged on lands or tenements`
    THEN `by suit against the person entitled to the entire rent, not against the tenant or the land`
    ELSE `the same remedies as for the entire portion`
